//   2026-07-08.15 · Workbook cell right-click: the "Delete cells" submenu now actually deletes cells. Previously it only offered Clear contents / Clear formatting (which leave the cell in place); it now leads with "Delete cells and shift up" and "Delete cells and shift left" — the Google Sheets behaviour that removes the selected cells (contents + formatting) and pulls the remaining cells in each affected column/row back over the gap, within the selected band only. New deleteCellsShift(g, dir) in workbook.js wired into both cell-menu handler switches; Clear contents / Clear formatting kept below a divider.
//   2026-07-08.16 · HOTFIX (regression from .12): Smart Fill wrote the shifts but the grid didn't repaint until you navigated away and back. The post-run solve-summary card's builder referenced sfPayload / _maxDaysOut, which are block-scoped inside autoAssignDriversForWeek and out of scope at that point → a ReferenceError threw out of autoAssignDriversForWeek → autoFillScheduleWeek never reached its post-write renderScheduleWeek(). The shifts were already saved (hence they showed on next navigation). Fix: build the recap from window._rrLastSmartFillPayload + function-scoped result/engineShifts/assigned, fully wrapped in try/catch so a broken recap can NEVER stop the render; hoist `summary` to function scope so the post-render card display resolves it; and show the card from inside autoFillScheduleWeek instead of a return value it never provided. live.js only.
//   2026-07-08.19 · Auto-build dashboards read cleaner. Three quality fixes in autoBuildDashboard (workbook.js): (1) KPI tiles now rank each sheet's numeric columns by real signal (non-zero total or >1 distinct value) before picking headline/secondary metrics, so dead "0" tiles like Worked=0 give way to a metric that actually moves; (2) sparklines are drawn only when a column has ≥3 non-null values with variation, killing the one-blip square-wave sparks on sparse columns; (3) charts are emitted only when they render legibly — a date dimension → a line over time, a categorical dimension only when it's low-cardinality (≤12 distinct) over ≤26 rows, otherwise no chart (a broken 66-bar smear was worse than none) — and series are capped at 4 (was up to 10) so the legend stays clean. workbook.js only.
//   2026-07-08.20 · Reports: forms become reportable. The Reports builder gains a "Forms" category — pick any of your forms and its submissions turn into a report (one row per submission, one column per field) with the same Download CSV / Print / Open in Workbook + Live-refresh options as People/Schedule/Attendance. Columns are built dynamically from the chosen form's fields (section headers / dividers / instructions excluded); driver + submitted-at + review status/flag/notes are fixed columns; signatures/photos/GPS/files collapse to readable text. Live report workbooks rebuild their catalog from get_form so they refresh even in a fresh session. reports.js only (RB_CATEGORIES + a dynamic "forms" source, fetchFormsList/fetchFormSubmissionsData, formsCatalogFrom, buildReportData forms branch, form-picker UI).
//   2026-07-08.21 · My Tasks recurrence gains a "Repeat forever" option — a no-end-date checkbox on the count row (hides the "for N occurrences" controls when on). Materialized-occurrence model has no rule-based top-up and the store caps a series at 60, so forever lays down the longest rolling horizon it can (60 occurrences) across whatever cadence is picked. view-schedule.frag + live.js + schedule-rrx.css.
//   2026-07-08.22 · "Repeat forever" tasks now auto-extend instead of running dry at the 60-occurrence seed. Forever series carry their recurrence rule in meta.recur (cadence + weekday/monthly pattern), and a daily pg_cron sweep (migration 0448) keeps each series topped up to a rolling buffer of ~40 future occurrences (refills when it drops below 20), resuming dormant series in the future with the weekday/day-of-month phase preserved. Count-limited repeats never set meta.recur, so the sweep skips them. live.js (recur rule on create) + migration 0448.
//   2026-07-08.23 · Workbook widget dialogs (Add table / KPI / chart): range fields get a "select on the grid" button so you no longer have to hand-type A1:K67. Clicking it drops the dialog out of the way, lets you drag across cells — and switch sheets via the tabs — with a floating bar showing the live range (Sheet!A1:K67) and Use-this-range / Cancel; Escape cancels, Enter confirms. Cross-sheet picks update the dialog's Data-source select; the dialog always returns to the sheet it was opened on so the widget still saves there. New wbRangeField/wireRangePick/beginRangePick+bar in workbook.js, hooked off paintSelection; styles in inline-styles.css.
//   2026-07-08.24 · Dashboard lockout escape hatch. Operators wedged on a stale app shell (an old service worker / Cache-Storage entries pointing at ?v= assets that no longer resolve) sat forever on the "We couldn't load your data" card — a view partial 404'd and boot aborted before __RR_BOOT_OK, and the card's only real action ("Try again") was a plain reload that re-served the same stale shell. Three fixes in index.html: (1) a shared window.__rrPurgeAndReload() that unregisters every SW AND deletes every Cache-Storage bucket, then hard-reloads uncontrolled with a cache-buster (self-timeout so a hung purge can't strand the operator); (2) the one-time auto-reset is re-armed (bumped key rr-sw-reset-20260708-dashlockout) and now purges Cache Storage too, not just SW registrations, so wedged clients self-heal on next load; (3) a "Reset app" button on the failure card plus an on-screen boot-error breadcrumb (window.__RR_BOOT_ERR) so the generic "connection hiccup" becomes an actionable detail line. index.html + inline-styles.css.
//   2026-07-08.25 · Workbook widget dialogs: switching the Data-source (sheet) dropdown now auto-fills the range field to that sheet's populated extent, so moving between sheets no longer leaves a stale range (e.g. A1:C66 from the old sheet). Only overwrites a range the user hasn't hand-edited — a range still holding the previous sheet's auto default gets refreshed; a deliberately typed one is left alone. New wireSrcAutoRange() in workbook.js wired into the chart / KPI / table dialogs.
//   2026-07-09.1 · Workbook reports: form photos render inline (PR #3627) and gain a right-click "Resize photos" tool with Small/Medium/Large/Extra-large presets + custom size (PR #3628). Both shipped as JS-only workbook.js/reports.js changes and never bumped this nonce, so pinned/installed/resumed apps kept the pre-feature bundle — photos still showed as "image.jpg" and the resize menu never appeared. Bumping forces every open window to the fresh shell so both features actually load.
//   2026-07-09.2 · Schedule enterprise polish pass. A visual-maturity re-skin of the Schedule dashboard toward a production logistics look: dark Amazon-style left rail (deep blue-black gradient, icon-over-label nav, bright-blue active accent bar), non-colorful app launcher/logo (monoline mark on a dark tile; the warm-yellow launcher button + colorful app-icon retired), darker top tabs with a blue active underline and a hairline divider under the whole row, a standardized 40px toolbar button system (white fill, #D7DEE8 hairline, 8px radius), an orange Finalize primary with a lock glyph (was blue), a compact coverage stat, a plain slate right utility rail (per-tile blue/violet/green/gold/indigo/teal/pink hues retired; blue only on the open panel), a subtle red-outline "V" van badge (was a solid red disc), and restrained light-blue shift cards (#F3F8FF + #CEDBEA hairline). CSS/markup only — inline-styles.css, schedule-rrx.css, index.html.
//   2026-07-09.3 · Desktop (window-controls-overlay) title bar: the segment above the dark left rail now paints the rail navy instead of light rail-blue, so the sidebar reads as reaching the very top of the window. Two-tone band with a hard colour stop at the rail width (84px collapsed / 176px expanded); the content-side segment stays light. inline-styles.css only.
//   2026-07-09.4 · HOTFIX — workbook cell right-click submenus (Resize photos, Paste special, Insert cells, Delete cells, View more cell actions) opened and instantly closed, so "Resize photos" never showed its size options. Drilling into a submenu re-renders the menu's innerHTML, detaching the clicked node; the document-level "click outside a popover closes it" handler then saw a target with no .wb-ctx-menu ancestor and wrongly closed the whole menu. Fixed by stopping propagation on clicks inside the cell menu. workbook.js only — nonce bump so installed/resumed apps get the fix.
//   2026-07-09.5 · Receipts panel: reconcile from the panel. Each still-open receipt card (Unreconciled / Needs Review / Duplicate Possible) gains an inline "Reconcile" action that marks it Matched in one click via the same receipt_set_status RPC the detail modal uses — the 0436 trigger mirrors it into the Receipt Ledger. Already-resolved receipts (Matched / Reimbursable) show no button; the detail modal still owns Reject / Reimbursable / notes. live.js + schedule-rrx.css.
//   2026-07-09.6 · Installed PWA (e.g. ChromeOS) title bar: theme-color → dark rail navy (#071E2F). In plain standalone display mode there is no window-controls-overlay band, so Chrome paints the whole window title bar from theme-color; it was light rail-blue, so the dark sidebar appeared to start below the top of the window. Navy theme-color makes the title bar dark (Chrome auto-lightens the min/max/close glyphs), so the rail reads as reaching the very top. manifest.webmanifest + index.html <meta theme-color>.
//   2026-07-09.7 · Receipts panel: close the dead space under the header. The filter toolbar (All / Needs review / Unreconciled / Duplicates chips + summary) defaults to opacity:0 and each panel re-reveals it via its own #id.is-open rule; Receipts was missing that rule, so the chips stayed invisible while still occupying ~113px — an empty band that pushed the first receipt far below the header. Added the receipts reveal rule so the toolbar shows and the list sits right under the header. schedule-rrx.css only.
//   2026-07-09.8 · Workbook report photos load fast at scale. A report can carry hundreds of photos; the grid is virtualized, so only on-screen photo cells now sign + fetch (lazy, per-viewport, concurrency-capped) instead of signing the whole sheet up front, and each thumbnail is served DOWNSCALED via Supabase image transforms (~640px, ~20KB) rather than the full ~1600px upload (~300KB) — a ~15× bytes cut — with loading=lazy + decoding=async. Click-to-enlarge signs a full-resolution URL on demand. If the storage tier can't transform, the first thumbnail 404s and we fall back to full-size URLs for the rest. workbook.js only.
//   2026-07-09.9 · Revert the full-width dark title bar (#3635 / nonce .6). Making theme-color navy turned the WHOLE standalone title bar dark, not just the sidebar column — a heavy full-width band. The sidebar-only-dark look is a two-tone effect that only works in Window Controls Overlay mode (body::before paints navy over the rail width, light over the content); standalone mode has one solid OS title bar, so theme-color goes back to light rail-blue (#EFF6FF) for a calm band there. WCO windows keep the sidebar-only navy. index.html <meta theme-color> + manifest.webmanifest theme_color (manifest ?v= bumped).
//   2026-07-09.10 · Workbook report photos: load the small thumbnail the driver app now uploads alongside each photo (answers[fid].thumb) instead of the full image — light loads on any Supabase tier (no server-side image transform, which is Pro-only). Photos with no thumbnail (older submissions, or a failed thumb upload) fall back to the full image; click-to-enlarge always signs the full-resolution URL; a missing thumbnail's <img> error swaps in the full image. Replaces the earlier transform-based approach. workbook.js + reports.js.
//   2026-07-09.11 · Schedule right utility rail: uniform filled icons. The last three rail buttons (Forms, Receipts, Recognition) were outline glyphs while the first five (Notes, My Tasks, Checklists, Contacts, Operations Health) were filled — a mismatched set. Redrew the three as filled glyphs (solid currentColor shape + white interior mark, matching the Checklists/Ops-Health style) so the whole rail reads as one system. view-schedule.frag only.
